{{% author %}}By Lanabi La Lova{{% /author %}} 

We follow the same template as the [previous page](/multilingual/english-german): tokenise, remove stopwords, then keep only the relevant script's characters. After tokenisation, we remove so-called "stopwords" using either `stopwords("ru", source = "snowball")` or `stopwords("ru", source = "marimo")`. [Marimo](https://github.com/koheiw/marimo) is larger and better suited to multilingual analysis than Snowball. If you want tokens to consist only of Cyrillic script, you can select them with the pattern `"^[а-яА-Я]+$"`, which lists the Cyrillic letter ranges directly rather than using a Unicode script class.

```{r, message=FALSE}
library(quanteda)
library(quanteda.corpora)
```

```{r include=FALSE}
options(width = 110)
```

{{% notice warning %}}
Be careful when pre-processing or removing tokens: as @denny2018 show, these choices might influence subsequent results, and the choice between the Snowball and Marimo stopword lists above is exactly this kind of decision.
{{% /notice %}}

```{r}
# reshape corpus to the level of paragraphs
corp_ru <- corpus_reshape(data_corpus_udhr["rus"], to = "paragraphs")

# tokenize corpus and apply pre-processing
toks_ru <- tokens(corp_ru, remove_punct = TRUE, remove_numbers = TRUE) |> 
  tokens_remove(pattern = stopwords("ru", source = "snowball")) 
print(toks_ru[2], max_ndoc = 1, max_ntoken = -1)
```

```{r}
# construct a document-feature matrix
# note that now all the features are converted to lowercase by default
dfmat_ru <- dfm(toks_ru)
print(dfmat_ru)
```

